# --- s052 Book I d.17 q.2
n("n828","s052-01","vtrum caritas vel quicunque habitus sit principium actiuum respectu actus","la","source","Compare Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 qq. 1–2, on whether the habit is an active principle of the act and on the opinions of Henry and Godfrey.","to-verify")
n("n829","s052-01","nihil prodest sine caritate infusa","la","intertext","1 Cor 13:3 (*caritatem autem non habuero, nihil mihi prodest*). The word *infusa* is Gorris's gloss.","certain")
n("n830","s052-01","machabeorum. 15.","la","intertext","2 Mcc 15:12–14 (Onias and Jeremiah, *fratrum amator*). B's numeral is not clearly legible in the reproduction (*13*?).","to-verify")
n("n831","s052-02","habitus est quo habens vtitur cum voluerit","la","source","Averroes, *In De anima* III comm. 18, which has the well-known definition *habitus est quo quis agit cum voluerit*.","to-verify")
n("n832","s052-03","opinio sustentabilis","la","doctrinal","The fourth opinion, that power and habit are partial causes concurring in one total cause with the power as the principal cause, is Scotus's own (*Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 qq. 1–2). Gorris presents it only as *sustentabilis*.","to-verify")
n("n833","s052-03","habitus nullam actionem vel actualitatem habet","la","source","The bearer of this opinion, that the habit only inclines the power, is not named by Gorris and has not been identified.","to-verify")
n("n834","s052-05","ad bene recipiendum actus","la","textual","Thus A. B reads *ad bene eliciendum actus*, which fits the habit as a principle of eliciting acts and the Doctor's reply (*concausandum*). The translation follows A but the reading of B is probably original.","verified")
n("n835","s052-05","dicit doctor sanctus","la","historical","Thomas Aquinas. The specific locus has not been identified; compare *ST* I-II q. 49 a. 3 and q. 50.","to-verify")
n("n836","s052-06","propter acceptationem actus nostri","la","doctrinal","Scotus's characteristic thesis that infused charity is required by divine ordination for the *acceptatio* of the act as meritorious, not because the act's goodness would otherwise be lacking. Compare *Ord.* I d. 17 p. 1 qq. 1–2.","to-verify")
n("n837","s052-06","nemo scit an odio vel amore dignus sit","la","intertext","Eccl 9:1 (*nescit homo utrum amore an odio dignus sit*).","certain")
n("n838","s052-06","dicit quidam","la","source","*Quidam* (knowledge of grace by faith through the sacraments) has not been identified.","to-verify")
n("n839","s052-07","⟨vita⟩","la","textual","A appears to print *via* (the *t* is not visible in the reproduction); *vita* is required (*merito pro vita eterna*). B reads *vita* (leaf 71r).","verified")
n("n840","s052-06","[and] that to those who have intense love","en","translation","The Latin juxtaposes two clauses without a conjunction (*… vt in baptismo illis qui habent dilectionem intensam quod deus dimittit delicta*). The sense is that sins are forgiven to those with intense love (cf. Lk 7:47).")
n("n841","s052-03","He rejects it","en","translation","*improbat*: the subject is the Doctor (Scotus), whose rejection is reported.")

